From srs at io.harvard.edu  Sat Oct 16 16:17:56 1999
From: srs at io.harvard.edu (Scott Saleska)
Date: Sat, 16 Oct 1999 12:17:56 -0400
Subject: Re: [S] multiple R-squared values
In-Reply-To: <Pine.OSF.4.10.9910151525380.775-100000@unicorn.it.wsu.edu>
References: <Pine.OSF.4.10.9910151525380.775-100000@unicorn.it.wsu.edu>
Message-ID: <3.0.3.32.19991016121756.00960970@pop.fas.harvard.edu>

For a model y~ f(x), the simple coefficient of determination (R-squared) is:

R2 = SS(model)/SS(total) 
= 1-SS(error)/SS(total) 
= 1 - sum((y-y.fit)^2)/(sum((y-y.null)^2)

where y.fit is fitted values and y.null usually = mean(y) (i.e. null model
for predicting y is the mean of y).  When the constant is omitted (i.e.
forced through zero) the null model is taken to be y=0, so y.null=0.  So
the reported total sum-of-squares (SS(total)) -- and hence R2 -- is always
bigger in Splus when the constant is omitted.  

This can be kind of confusing since the variation explained by a model with
*no* intercept (relative to mean(y) by itself) is always <= variation
explained by model *with* an intercept.  

-scott

