Subject: [S] Summary: Splus resources for glm models for count data.
Message-ID: <19991017204615.16269.rocketmail@web217.mail.yahoo.com>

Summary: Splus resources for glm models for count
data.

For the analysis of count data, the Splus glm function
offers, at least, three useful alternatives,
analyzable in three main forms: Poisson, negative
binomial, and quasi (with a log-link & a mu-variance).
The negative binomial option is available in the
glm.nb function of the Venables & Ripley mass library.
The glm.nb and the quasi-log-mu are useful for
overdispersed data. Warning: As of this writing, the
glm.nb & summary.negbin for the mass library in
Splus2000 for windows are printing incorrect results.
Earlier versions (e.g., Splus4.5) should be used
instead.

Various glm functions are available from Thomas Yee
<yee at scitec.auckland.ac.nz>  at 
http://www.stat.auckland.ac.nz/yee/vgam
These models are restricted to the UNIX environment.

General information on glm models is available in the
very elaborate and useful ?glm? web site that is
maintained by Gordon K. Smyth in
http://www.maths.uq.oz.au/~gks/research/glm

The most promising innovations in this field are being
produced by Michael J. Faddy, Heather M. Podlich, and
Gordon K. Smyth in the analysis of Extended Poisson
Process Models (EPPMs). Downloadable Splus Software
(for Splus3.3 for windows), a PhD thesis, and
manuscripts on EPPMs are available from Heather M.
Podlich?s web site in 
http://www.maths.uq.edu.au/~hmp

I received Splus codes for Zero Inflated Poisson (ZIP)
and Zero Inflated Negative Binomial (ZINB1, ZINB), and
a two parameter negative binomial density from Peter
Perkins at peter at caliban.ucsd.edu.  
I also received a ZIP code from Diane Lambert at 
dl at research.bell-labs.com

For their useful responses to my query, I thank,
Heather Podlich, Andy White, Thomas Yee, Bill
Venables, Gordon, K. Smyth, Timo Makelainen, Peter
Perkins, Chris Barker, John Leathwick, Richard Carson,
and Diane Lambert.
  
If you are familiar with other Splus resources for the
analysis of count data, please let me know.

 

