> binomial, and quasi (with a log-link & a mu-variance).
> The negative binomial option is available in the
> glm.nb function of the Venables & Ripley mass library.
> The glm.nb and the quasi-log-mu are useful for
> overdispersed data. Warning: As of this writing, the
> glm.nb & summary.negbin for the mass library in
> Splus2000 for windows are printing incorrect results.
> Earlier versions (e.g., Splus4.5) should be used
> instead.

As the error is in S-PLUS 2000 and not the MASS library, it affects almost
all uses of make.family (and that probably includes the use of quasi in
this context). The results are calculated incorrectly, not just printed
incorrectly.  The fix is to revert the function glm.weight to

glm.weight <- function(link, variance)
{
   default <- expression(w/((sqrt(family$variance(mu))*family$deriv(mu))^2))
   dnames <- dimnames(glm.weights)
   if(!match(link, dnames[[1]], F)) return(default)
   if(!match(variance, dnames[[2]], F))  return(default)
   ww <- glm.weights[link, variance]
   if(as.character(ww) == "NULL") default
   else ww
}

I trust Mathsoft have now learnt to run the test suites after making
last-minute changes!  I have been assured that this will be corrected in a
forthcoming patch.

