From therneau at mayo.edu  Mon Oct 18 13:12:06 1999
From: therneau at mayo.edu (Therneau, Terry M., Ph.D.)
Date: Mon, 18 Oct 1999 08:12:06 -0500
Subject: Re: [S] Survival curves and actuarial survivals
Message-ID: <199910181312.IAA28366@rocky.Mayo.EDU>

 At least in the medical world, there seems to be a lot of confusion here
and I get asked for "actuarial" survival all the time.  There are two meanings
for "actuarial survival":

 1. Survival curves computed by the actuarial method.  These are useful for
grouped data, i.e., one knows the number who started, and the number who
withdrew (lost to follow-up) and the number who died each year, but not the
individual data for each individual subject.  There was a query in S-news 
not too long ago about computing curves for a fruit fly experiment, for
instance.
    There are several different 'actuarial' estimators; they vary in the 
assumptions made about the withdrawals, but the answers vary little.  None
of the methods are part of the Splus.

  2. Survival curves computed by whatever method, with "actuarial" as an
empty adjective.  This is what my customers almost always want.   Often,
the value of the curve at 1, 2, 3, ... years is being asked for; perhaps this
is the source of confusion since that is the natural output of an actuarial
calculation.  To get the values at specific time points, use summary():

> fit <- survfit(Surv(time, status) ~ ph.ecog, data=lung)
> summary(fit, times= 0:4 * 365)
Call: survfit(formula = Surv(time, status) ~ ph.ecog, data = lung)
 
