From David-Smith at ouhsc.edu  Mon Oct 18 13:48:35 1999
From: David-Smith at ouhsc.edu (David W. Smith)
Date: Mon, 18 Oct 1999 08:48:35 -0500
Subject: RE: [S] Survival curves and actuarial survivals
Message-ID: <BABD715C8EE0D211B7F30000F8033E5602124494@email.ouhsc.edu>

Historically, the actuarial method is distinguished from the Kaplan-Meier
method.  I believe that the actuarial method was first introduced and named
in an article in the Proceedings of the Staff Meetings of the Mayo Clinic in
1947.  It was called the actuarial method because it used the method of
computation usually used by actuaries for a life table for a large
population.  The difference was that the starting time was usually taken as
the time the disease was diagnosed.  Some years later (I think in the
mid-50's) Kaplan and Meier proposed their method, using more exact times of
death or failure, as a technique more suited to small samples, which it
generally is, in an article in the Journal of Chronic Diseases.  Both
methods give an estimate of the survival rate at each time after entry to
the study.  An actuarial estimate of a life table doesn't require that a
single group of subjects be followed continuously, since it can be
constructed from death rates for the specific intervals.  This is the way a
life table for a population, such as the United States, is constructed,
using death rates by age for a relatively short time interval such as one
year or three years.

I have had different clients insist on each of these methods, actuarial and
Kaplan-Meier.  Usually, I think, because it was what was used in an article
they had seen recently.  

I doubt that most people know the difference between them any more.
Moreover, the term "actuarial method" may have seemed rather odd to the
actuaries, who started all their life tables at birth rather than with the
varying ages of onset of a disease.

Cordially,
David Smith

