In response to a query about obtaining actuarial survivals, Dr. Therneau
distinguished two senses of "actuarial", the first being . . .

> 1. Survival curves computed by the actuarial method.  These are useful for
>grouped data, i.e., one knows the number who started, and the number who
>withdrew (lost to follow-up) and the number who died each year, but not the
>individual data for each individual subject.  There was a query in S-news 
>not too long ago about computing curves for a fruit fly experiment, for
>instance.
>    There are several different 'actuarial' estimators; they vary in the 
>assumptions made about the withdrawals, but the answers vary little.  None
>of the methods are part of the Splus.

While it is true that these methods are not explicitly part of S-Plus,
perhaps Dr. Therneau is being modest in not mentioning that the Splus core
function survfit() that he authored can indeed be used to analyze grouped
data in essentially an actuarial, or life-table manner.   The survfit()
weights=  argument is the key: set it equal to the number of events
associated with each row of data.  Create two data rows for any time
interval during which both deaths and losses-to-followup occur.  Various
assumptions about withdrawals merely require appropriate adjustments in the
'time' column for those rows recording withdrawals.

