From ecm at casbah.acns.nwu.edu  Mon Oct 18 19:49:06 1999
From: ecm at casbah.acns.nwu.edu (Edward Malthouse)
Date: Mon, 18 Oct 1999 14:49:06 -0500
Subject: [S] Quantiles and consistency (fwd)
Message-ID: <199910181949.OAA09641@casbah.acns.nwu.edu>

The big question here is how to define a quantile of a list of
numbers.  There are several definitions floating around and I'd like
to hear the opinions of other reads on the list on this subject. 
The results I report below are computed using Splus2000 under NT.
According to the help page for quantile, "The algorithm linearly
interpolates between order statistics of x, assuming that the ith
order statistic is the (i-1)/(length(x)-1) quantile."  The help page
for stem makes no mention of the linear interpolation.  

Tukey's EDA book does not define the term quartile; instead it
defines a "hinge" on page 33, which has roughly the same meaning as
quartile ("fold" the data twice and the three bends are the hinges
and median (Tukey does use the term median)).

I used to teach from a book (Madsen and Mushbuger (sp?)) that
defined the first quartile Q1 as a number such that at least 25% of
the numbers are <= Q1 AND at least 75% of the number are >= Q1.
Note that using this definition 69.75 (from quantile) is not a
quartile since
> sum(dat>=69.75)/length(dat)
[1] 0.7333333
and .73<.75
Note also that 82 (from stem) is not a third quartile by this
definition because
> sum(dat>=82)/length(dat)
[1] 0.2333333
SAS proc univariate gives Q1=69 and Q3=81, which satisfy this
definition.

In the case of a continuous random variable with a monotonic CDF
there is no problem in defining quantiles.  Problems arise when the
CDF is not monotonic, as is the case with discrete data.  My
preference is to stick with the Madsen and Mushbuger definition
because it fits the notion that the quartiles divide the list into
fourths.  How can 82 be a value of Q3 when less than 25% of the data
are greater than or equal to 82?

I imagine that the particular method used to compute quartiles is
not important as long as the analyst is consistent.

Ed Malthouse

