Several of you pointed out a problem in my previous mailing.  I had
mistyped the numbers.  Please forgive my mistake.  Let me give a
shorter example that makes the same point:

> sort(x)
 [1]  0  0  3  5  7  9  9 14 23 23 24 25 28 30 34
> stem(x)

N = 15   Median = 14
Quartiles = 5, 25

Decimal point is 1 place to the right of the colon

   0 : zz35799
   1 : 4
   2 : 33458
   3 : 04

> quantile(x)
   0%  25%  50%  75% 100% 
    0    6   14 24.5   34
> sum(x>=6)/length(x)
[1] 0.7333333
> sum(x<=24.5)/length(x)
[1] 0.7333333

How can 6 be a first quartile when less than 75% of the
distribution is >= 6?  Likewise, how can 24.5 be Q3 when less than
75% of the distribution is <=24.5?  Proc univariate and SPSS give t
e same answer as stem.

Ed Malthouse
